    stages: [{"id":"twist","title":"The twist — streaming + batch, <50ms, point-in-time","ask":"Design a point-in-time-correct pipeline mixing streaming and batch features for a model responding in <50ms. Own it.","attemptHint":"Two feature velocities, one correctness rule, a tight budget.","model":"Serve from a low-latency online store (<50ms reads), populate it from streaming (real-time aggregates) and batch (historical) computed by the SAME definitions used in training, and enforce point-in-time correctness so training reconstructs exactly what serving would have had at each timestamp. Watermarks/late-data handling keep streaming features consistent; skew monitoring guards it.","heuristic":"Mixed streaming/batch features must share definitions and be reconstructable as-of any time; serve from a fast online store.","control":"Read latency; streaming/batch point-in-time consistency.","trap":"Streaming features that can't be reconstructed for training (skew) or that leak future aggregates.","tell":"Live features the training pipeline can't reproduce as-of the prediction time.","anchors":[{"dim":"streaming-pit","anchor":"how do streaming features stay point-in-time correct (event-time, no future leakage)?","cost":"streaming introduces subtle future leakage"},{"dim":"latency","anchor":"does the online feature fetch + score fit under 50ms (precompute vs on-demand)?","cost":"feature fetch blows the latency budget"},{"dim":"consistency","anchor":"how are streaming + batch features made consistent at training and serving?","cost":"mixed sources reintroduce skew"},{"dim":"tradeoff","anchor":"freshness vs latency vs cost stated with the deciding factor?","cost":"reads as no real decision"}]}],
