    stages: [{"id":"twist","title":"The twist — GBDT plateaued, signal in IDs/text","ask":"Your GBDT plateaued and the signal is in high-cardinality IDs and text you're not using. Decide what to change. Minimal scaffold.","attemptHint":"Now DL might genuinely win. Why here specifically?","model":"Here DL earns it: high-cardinality IDs want learned EMBEDDINGS and text wants a language model — structure GBDT handles poorly. Bring those modalities in (embeddings for IDs, text encoders), possibly fusing with the tabular features (a hybrid), and prove the lift comes from the newly-used signal. The trigger is the data structure, not dissatisfaction with GBDT.","heuristic":"High-cardinality IDs and text are the structural triggers where embeddings/DL beat GBDT.","control":"Lift specifically attributable to embeddings/text vs the GBDT baseline.","trap":"Either staying on GBDT while ignoring the text/ID signal, or switching to DL without attributing the lift.","tell":"The plateau breaks only once the unused high-cardinality/text signal is modeled.","anchors":[{"dim":"diagnose-limit","anchor":"do you identify high-cardinality/text as where GBDT struggles (representation)?","cost":"you keep tuning GBDT past its ceiling"},{"dim":"embeddings","anchor":"embeddings / DL for the sparse/text signal (possibly fused with GBDT)?","cost":"the real signal stays unused"},{"dim":"hybrid","anchor":"do you consider a hybrid (embeddings as features into GBDT, or DLRM)?","cost":"an all-or-nothing swap ignores GBDT's remaining value"},{"dim":"prove-gain","anchor":"do you validate the DL/embedding gain beats the GBDT baseline on metric AND cost?","cost":"you adopt DL without proving it earns its cost"}]}],
