    prompt: "Variation of the classical root (own it — no scaffold): the use case is credit/lending (regulated) — you must explain every decision. Choose and justify the model and the explanation approach.",
    context: "You get the constraint only. Bring your own model + interpretability design.",
    produce: { artifact: "the model choice for a regulated decision + the explanation approach (SHAP/reason codes/monotonic constraints) + how you satisfy the regulator + tradeoffs", format: "design-doc", workspace: "in-app-text" },
    stages: [{"id":"twist","title":"The twist — regulated credit/lending, explain every decision","ask":"Credit/lending (regulated) — you must explain every decision. Choose the model and explanation approach. Own it.","attemptHint":"Interpretability is a hard requirement here. What does that constrain?","model":"In regulated lending, explainability is a hard requirement (adverse-action reasons, fair-lending audits). Favor inherently interpretable or well-explainable models — a monotonic-constrained GBDT or a regularized GLM/scorecard — with SHAP/reason-code generation, monotonicity constraints for sensible/defensible behavior, and documented fairness testing. A black box you can't explain per-decision is disqualified regardless of accuracy.","heuristic":"Regulated decisions require per-decision explanations; pick a model whose explanations are defensible, not just accurate.","control":"Reason-code coverage; fairness/adverse-action compliance.","trap":"An unexplainable black box because it scored slightly higher.","tell":"A decision you can't justify to a regulator or the applicant.","anchors":[{"dim":"explainable-choice","anchor":"do you choose a model you can explain per-decision (GBDT+SHAP / monotonic / GLM), not a black box?","cost":"an unexplainable model fails compliance (ECOA/reason codes)"},{"dim":"reason-codes","anchor":"can you produce adverse-action reason codes per decision?","cost":"you can't legally deny credit without them"},{"dim":"monotonic","anchor":"do you use monotonic constraints where the direction is required (e.g. more debt -> higher risk)?","cost":"counter-intuitive, indefensible decisions"},{"dim":"tradeoff","anchor":"accuracy vs interpretability stated for the regulated context?","cost":"reads as no real decision"}]}],
